{
  "id": "03_fortran_legacy",
  "retired": "2026-09-19. The premise is false. The code as published returns 1.5, not 1.0: Fortran's implicit rule types SUM as REAL (S is not in I-N), so RES = SUM / N is real division and the stated symptom never occurs. Verified with gfortran -std=legacy; renaming SUM to NSUM (implicit INTEGER) does reproduce 1.0. The rubric carried the contradiction openly - 'SUM is implicitly REAL' next to 'recognises the integer division' - and nobody read the two lines together. Thirteen of fourteen models were marked as passing, because contains_any matched the word 'implicit' and never checked whether the reasoning held. Found by writing a known-good answer for it, which is the whole point of evals/oracle.py. Replaced by 11_fortran_legacy, which names the variable so the bug is real.",
  "title": "Old Fortran, silent error",
  "why": "Legacy modernisation is real work, and this bug is the classic one in it: implicit typing.",
  "profile": [
    "analysis"
  ],
  "check": "contains_any",
  "expect": [
    "implicit",
    "IMPLICIT",
    "Implicit",
    "integer",
    "INTEGER",
    "Integer"
  ],
  "rubric": [
    "Finds that N and I are implicitly INTEGER while SUM is implicitly REAL",
    "Recognises the integer division in forming the mean",
    "Names IMPLICIT NONE as the remedy"
  ],
  "prompt": "This Fortran 77 code is meant to compute a mean, but for X = [1.0, 2.0] it returns 1.0 instead of 1.5. Why?\n\n      SUBROUTINE MEAN(X, N, RES)\n      REAL X(N), RES\n      SUM = 0.0\n      DO 10 I = 1, N\n         SUM = SUM + X(I)\n   10 CONTINUE\n      RES = SUM / N\n      RETURN\n      END"
}
